{"abstract":"Urban highways are common, especially in the United States, making cities more car-centric. They promise the annihilation of distance but obstruct pedestrian mobility, thus playing a key role in limiting social interactions locally. Although this limiting role is widely acknowledged in urban studies, the quantitative relationship between urban highways and social ties is barely tested. Here, we define a Barrier Score that relates massive, geolocated online social network data to highways in the 50 largest US cities. At the granularity of individual social ties, we show that urban highways are associated with decreased social connectivity. This barrier effect is especially strong for short distances and consistent with historical cases of highways that were built to purposefully disrupt or isolate Black neighborhoods. By combining spatial infrastructure with social tie data, our method adds a dimension to demographic studies of social segregation. Our study can inform reparative planning for an evidence-based reduction of spatial inequality, and more generally, support a better integration of the social fabric in urban planning.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Aiello, Luca Maria","Vybornova, Anastassia","Juhász, Sándor","Szell, Michael","Bokányi, Eszter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808930"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Hungary","Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Corvinus University of Budapest","University of Amsterdam"],"keyword":["Highways","Segregation","Social media","Social network","Urban Computing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"urban highways are barriers to social ties","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109113511/pnas25urban.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["L.M.A. acknowledges funding from Carlsberg Foundation Project COCOONS (Grant ID: CF210432). M.S. acknowledges funding from European Union Horizon Project JUST STREETS (Grant agreement ID: 101104240). S.J. acknowledges funding from EU Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellowship Programme (Grant number 101062606)"],"id":"S01-000.00-808930","identifier":[{"id":"86000234747","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ff25188e-1a00-458c-9cc9-495a1baf903e"],"keyword":["Urban Computing","barrier effect","highways","segregation","social media","social network"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-D10120BD7B14D1FC30858A70C048E65F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109113511/pnas25urban.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.11596","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, MSc in Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"hu","key":"corvinus university of budapest","loc":["Hungary","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Corvinus University of Budapest","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"university of amsterdam","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Amsterdam","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","locid":"60b2482e-a0d3-4094-8723-76ea44a33c30","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","orcid":"0000-0001-6915-2561","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sándor","key":"juhászpau","last":"Juhász","name":"Juhász, Sándor","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Eszter","key":"bokányipau","last":"Bokányi","name":"Bokányi, Eszter","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2404.11596","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"issue":"10","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","paperid":"e2408937122","publisher":"The National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","volume":"122"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000234747"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ff25188e-1a00-458c-9cc9-495a1baf903e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Urban highways are barriers to social ties","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808930","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Social conventions are the backbone of social coordination, shaping how individuals form a group. As growing populations of artificial intelligence (AI) agents communicate through natural language, a fundamental question is whether they can bootstrap the foundations of a society. Here, we present experimental results that demonstrate the spontaneous emergence of universally adopted social conventions in decentralized populations of large language model (LLM) agents. We then show how strong collective biases can emerge during this process, even when agents exhibit no bias individually. Last, we examine how committed minority groups of adversarial LLM agents can drive social change by imposing alternative social conventions on the larger population. Our results show that AI systems can autonomously develop social conventions without explicit programming and have implications for designing AI systems that align, and remain aligned, with human values and societal goals.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Ashery, Ariel Flint","Aiello, Luca Maria","Baronchelli, Andrea"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-819741"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["Alan Turing Institute","All Global Affiliations","St. George's University of London"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"emergent social conventions and collective bias in llm populations","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adu9368","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-819741","identifier":[{"id":"105005476741","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12077490","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40367164","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-9c7717a9-8349-4091-8640-28b699e34273"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-CEBF13676D3811E2C4A3FFAC1EFCD484"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/451453204/Emergent_social_conventions.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adu9368","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"st george s university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"St. George's University of London","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"alan turing institute","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Alan Turing Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ariel Flint","key":"asherypau","last":"Ashery","name":"Ashery, Ariel Flint","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","country":"dk","first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"bddd0034-357e-4b8e-9586-9447314a8a67","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","first":"Andrea","key":"baronchellipau","last":"Baronchelli","name":"Baronchelli, Andrea","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40367164"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adu9368","issn":["23752548"],"issue":"20","journal":"Science Advances","paperid":"eadu9368","status":"p","title":"Science Advances","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105005476741"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9c7717a9-8349-4091-8640-28b699e34273"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Emergent social conventions and collective bias in LLM populations","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-819741","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Addressing the climate crisis calls for global efforts to mitigate greenhouse gas emissions, with effective mitigation efforts depending on an informed public with a high degree of climate change awareness. This study examines global engagement with climate change and related concepts through an analysis of around 517 Million Wikipedia pageviews of 3965 items from WikiProject Climate Change across 213 countries in the years 2017 to 2022. We take advantage of Wikimedia Foundation's differentially-private daily pageview dataset, which makes it possible to study Wikipedia viewing behavior in a language edition agnostic way and on a per-country basis. Temporal analysis reveals a stagnant engagement with climate change articles, contrary to societal trends, possibly due to the attitude-behavior gap. We also found substantial regional differences, with countries from the global north displaying greater traffic compared to the global south. Specific events, notably Greta Thunberg's speech at the UN climate summit in 2019, drive peaks in climate change engagement, highlighting the social dimension and influence of prominent figures in climate change information seeking. However, causal time series analyses show that events like these do not lead to long-lasting increased traffic.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Meier, Florian Maximilian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-717102"],"event":{"end":"2024-05-24","place":"Stuttgart, de","start":"2024-05-21","title":"16th ACM Web Science Conference","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Climate change","Public Interest","Time series analysis","Wikipedia"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"using wikipedia pageview data to investigate public interest in climate change at a global scale","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3614419.3644007","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-717102","identifier":[{"id":"85195143261","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-f99e36e3-ed7a-4980-8152-6d25adb51271"],"keyword":["Climate Change","Public Interest","TIme Series Analysis","Wikipedia"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DF25B4986CEFB0956D94366250254578"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/700708326/Meier_Wiki_Climate.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3614419.3644007","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Human Centered Science and Digital Technology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Purposeful Technology Lab","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, MASSHINE","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, AI for the People"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Florian Maximilian","key":"meierpau","last":"Meier","locid":"30784c17-7dc1-48de-9a36-f6e17f329de0","name":"Meier, Florian Maximilian","orcid":"0000-0001-9408-0686","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopedh","last":"Aiello","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Yelena","key":"mejovapedh","last":"Mejova","name":"Mejova, Yelena","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Oshani","key":"seneviratnepedh","last":"Seneviratne","name":"Seneviratne, Oshani","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jun","key":"sunpedh","last":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jun","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Sierra","key":"kaiserpedh","last":"Kaiser","name":"Kaiser, Sierra","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Steffen","key":"staabpedh","last":"Staab","name":"Staab, Steffen","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3614419.3644007","eisbn":["9798400703348"],"pages":"365-375","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)","status":"p","subtitle":"Proceedings of the 16th ACM Web Science Conference","title":"WebSci 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85195143261"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-f99e36e3-ed7a-4980-8152-6d25adb51271"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Using Wikipedia Pageview Data to Investigate Public Interest in Climate Change at a Global Scale","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-717102","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The migration of Twitter users to Mastodon following Elon Musk’s acquisition presents a unique opportunity to study collective behavior and gain insights into the drivers of coordinated behavior in online media. We analyzed the social network and the public conversations of about 75,000 migrated users and observed that the temporal trace of their migrations is compatible with a phenomenon of social influence, as described by a compartmental epidemic model of information diffusion. Drawing from prior research on behavioral change, we delved into the factors that account for variations of the effectiveness of the influence process across different Twitter communities. Communities in which the influence process unfolded more rapidly exhibit lower density of social connections, higher levels of signaled commitment to migrating, and more emphasis on shared identity and exchange of factual knowledge in the community discussion. These factors account collectively for 57% of the variance in the observed data. Our results highlight the joint importance of network structure, commitment, and psycho-linguistic aspects of social interactions in characterizing grassroots collective action, and contribute to deepen our understanding of the mechanisms that drive processes of behavior change of online groups.","author_count":3,"contributors":["La Cava, Lucio","Aiello, Luca Maria","Tagarelli, Andrea"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718875"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Calabria"],"keyword":["Computer Science","Information technology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"drivers of social influence in the twitter migration to mastodon","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-48200-7","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["LMA acknowledges the support from the Carlsberg Foundation through the COCOONS project (CF21-0432)."],"id":"S01-000.00-718875","identifier":[{"id":"85178871996","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-80ff22cf-fd99-47e0-864f-001ed74132c5"],"keyword":["Computer science","Information technology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1D39E48E99F5D7DEDAB48EB6F62ECC2F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-48200-7","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102753165/s41598-023-48200-7.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"university of calabria","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Calabria","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lucio","key":"lacavapau","last":"La Cava","name":"La Cava, Lucio","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrea","key":"tagarellipau","last":"Tagarelli","name":"Tagarelli, Andrea","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-023-48200-7","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"21626","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85178871996"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-80ff22cf-fd99-47e0-864f-001ed74132c5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Drivers of social influence in the Twitter migration to Mastodon","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718875","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"While sleep positively impacts well-being, health, and productivity, the effects of societal factors on sleep remain underexplored. Here we analyze the sleep of 30,082 individuals across 11 countries using 52 million activity records from wearable devices. Our data are consistent with past studies of gender and age-associated sleep characteristics. However, our analysis of wearable device data uncovers differences in recorded vs. self-reported bedtime and sleep duration. The dataset allowed us to study how country-specific metrics such as GDP and cultural indices relate to sleep in groups and individuals. Our analysis indicates that diverse sleep metrics can be represented by two dimensions: sleep quantity and quality. We find that 55% of the variation in sleep quality, and 63% in sleep quantity, are explained by societal factors. Within a societal boundary, individual sleep experience was modified by factors like exercise. Increased exercise or daily steps were associated with better sleep quality (for example, faster sleep onset and less time awake in bed), especially in countries like the U.S. and Finland. Understanding how social norms relate to sleep will help create strategies and policies that enhance the positive impacts of sleep on health, such as productivity and well-being.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Park, Sungkyu","Zhunis, Assem","Constantinides, Marios","Aiello, Luca Maria","Quercia, Daniele","Cha, Meeyoung"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718925"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["South Korea","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology","Nokia Bell Labs"],"keyword":["Human behaviour","Psychology and behaviour","Quality of Life"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"social dimensions impact individual sleep quantity and quality","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-36762-5","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was partially supported by the Carlsberg Foundation through the COCOONS project (CF21-0432)."],"id":"S01-000.00-718925","identifier":[{"id":"85162015504","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d9787fc8-c82e-4cc9-a596-e695728d6bab"],"keyword":["Human behaviour","Psychology and behaviour","Quality of life"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A643DB745566A4141BC212B594192CE3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-36762-5","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"kr","key":"korea advanced institute of science and technology","loc":["South Korea","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"nokia bell labs","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Nokia Bell Labs","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sungkyu","key":"parkpau","last":"Park","name":"Park, Sungkyu","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Assem","key":"zhunispau","last":"Zhunis","name":"Zhunis, Assem","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marios","key":"constantinidespau","last":"Constantinides","name":"Constantinides, Marios","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniele","key":"querciapau","last":"Quercia","name":"Quercia, Daniele","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Meeyoung","key":"chapau","last":"Cha","name":"Cha, Meeyoung","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-023-36762-5","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"9681","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85162015504"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d9787fc8-c82e-4cc9-a596-e695728d6bab"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Social dimensions impact individual sleep quantity and quality","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718925","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The strength of social relations has been shown to affect an individual's access to opportunities. To date, however, the correspondence between tie strength and population's economic prospects has not been quantified, largely because of the inability to operationalise strength based on Granovetter's classic theory. Our work departed from the premise that tie strength is a unidimensional construct (typically operationalized with frequency or volume of contact), and used instead a validated model of ten fundamental dimensions of social relationships grounded in the literature of social psychology. We built state-of-the-art NLP tools to infer the presence of these dimensions from textual communication, and analyzed a large conversation network of 630K geo-referenced Reddit users across the entire US connected by 12.8M social ties created over the span of 7 years. We found that unidimensional tie strength is only weakly correlated with economic opportunities (R2=0.30), while multidimensional constructs are highly correlated (R2=0.62). In particular, economic opportunities are associated to the combination of: i) knowledge ties, which bridge geographically distant groups, facilitating the knowledge dissemination across communities; and ii) social support ties, which knit geographically close communities together, and represent dependable sources of social and emotional support. These results point to the importance of developing high-quality measures of tie strength in network theory.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Aiello, Luca Maria","Joglekar, Sagar","Quercia, Daniele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381257"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nokia Bell Labs"],"keyword":["Economic development","Knowledge","NLP","Reddit","Social media","Social networks","Support"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multidimensional tie strength and economic development","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-26245-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381257","identifier":[{"id":"85144495067","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-00a78b46-d8bb-4840-8ddc-8110e37b0035"],"keyword":["NLP","economic development","knowledge","reddit","social media","social networks","support"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-35A738309D49F4AF6B8C7DDCD6842796"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-26245-4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/91571230/s41598_022_26245_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"nokia bell labs","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Nokia Bell Labs","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sagar","key":"joglekarpau","last":"Joglekar","name":"Joglekar, Sagar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniele","key":"querciapau","last":"Quercia","name":"Quercia, Daniele","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-022-26245-4","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","pages":"1-11","paperid":"22081","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85144495067"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-00a78b46-d8bb-4840-8ddc-8110e37b0035"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multidimensional Tie Strength and Economic Development","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381257","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The COVID-19 pandemic generated a considerable debate in relation to urban density. This is an old debate, originated in mid 19th century’s England with the emergence of public health and urban planning disciplines. While popularly linked, evidence suggests that such relationship cannot be generally assumed. Furthermore, urban density has been investigated in a spatially coarse manner (predominantly at city level) and never contextualised with other descriptors of urban form. In this work, we explore COVID-19 and urban form in Greater London, relating a comprehensive set of morphometric descriptors (including built-up density) to COVID-19 deaths and cases, while controlling for socioeconomic, ethnicity, age and co-morbidity. We describe urban form at individual building level and then aggregate information for official neighbourhoods, allowing for a detailed intra-urban representation. Results show that: (i) control variables significantly explain more variance of both COVID-19 cases and deaths than the morphometric descriptors; (ii) of what the latter can explain, built-up density is indeed the most associated, though inversely. The typical London neighbourhood with high levels of COVID-19 infections and deaths resembles a suburb, featuring a low-density urban fabric dotted by larger free-standing buildings and framed by a poorly inter-connected street network.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Venerandi, Alessandro","Aiello, Luca Maria","Porta, Sergio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384494"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Strathclyde"],"keyword":["Covid 19","London","Urban morphology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"urban form and covid 000000000019 cases and deaths in greater london an urban morphometric approach","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/23998083221133397","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384494","identifier":[{"id":"85139983811","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-db80f903-08d8-4b56-9863-5e0ba11d5ed4"],"keyword":["COVID-19","London","urban morphology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-08EA721157CD8D0F54E361C55C2782B1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90810929/23998083221133397.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/23998083221133397","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of strathclyde","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Strathclyde","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alessandro","key":"venerandipau","last":"Venerandi","name":"Venerandi, Alessandro","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sergio","key":"portapau","last":"Porta","name":"Porta, Sergio","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/23998083221133397","issn":["23998083","23998091"],"issue":"0","journal":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","pages":"1-16","paperid":"23998083221133397","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","volume":"0"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85139983811"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-db80f903-08d8-4b56-9863-5e0ba11d5ed4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Urban form and COVID-19 cases and deaths in Greater London: An urban morphometric approach","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384494","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Which messages are more effective at inducing a change of opinion in the listener? We approach this question within the frame of Habermas’ theory of communicative action, which posits that the illocutionary intent of the message (its pragmatic meaning) is the key. Thanks to recent advances in natural language processing, we are able to operationalize this theory by extracting the latent social dimensions of a message, namely archetypes of social intent of language, that come from social exchange theory. We identify key ingredients to opinion change by looking at more than 46k posts and more than 3.5M comments on Reddit’s r/ChangeMyView, a debate forum where people try to change each other’s opinion and explicitly mark opinion-changing comments with a special flag called delta. Comments that express no intent are about 77% less likely to change the mind of the recipient, compared to comments that convey at least one social dimension. Among the various social dimensions, the ones that are most likely to produce an opinion change are knowledge, similarity, and trust, which resonates with Habermas’ theory of communicative action. We also find other new important dimensions, such as appeals to power or empathetic expressions of support. Finally, in line with theories of constructive conflict, yet contrary to the popular characterization of conflict as the bane of modern social media, our findings show that voicing conflict in the context of a structured public debate can promote integration, especially when it is used to counter another conflictive stance. By leveraging recent advances in natural language processing, our work provides an empirical framework for Habermas’ theory, finds concrete examples of its effects in the wild, and suggests its possible extension with a more faceted understanding of intent interpreted as social dimensions of language.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Monti, Corrado","Aiello, Luca Maria","De Francisci Morales, Gianmarco","Bonchi, Francesco"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381555"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CENTAI"],"keyword":["Habermas","NLP","Reddit","Social dimensions","opinion change"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the language of opinion change on social media under the lens of communicative action","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-21720-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381555","identifier":[{"id":"85140631793","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-132b0f23-0637-4dd4-a1ac-fd1055d691c9"],"keyword":["NLP","habermas","opinion change","reddit","social dimensions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B2135F536F2F095431B21FE2EC32D8A6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90811579/s41598_022_21720_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-21720-4","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"centai","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"CENTAI","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Corrado","key":"montipau","last":"Monti","name":"Monti, Corrado","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gianmarco","key":"defranciscimoralespau","last":"De Francisci Morales","name":"De Francisci Morales, Gianmarco","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Francesco","key":"bonchipau","last":"Bonchi","name":"Bonchi, Francesco","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-022-21720-4","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","pages":"1-11","paperid":"17920","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85140631793"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-132b0f23-0637-4dd4-a1ac-fd1055d691c9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The language of opinion change on social media under the lens of communicative action","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381555","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In January 2021, retail investors coordinated on Reddit to target short-selling activity by hedge funds on GameStop shares, causing a surge in the share price and triggering significant losses for the funds involved. Such an effective collective action was unprecedented in finance, and its dynamics remain unclear. Here, we analyse Reddit and financial data and rationalize the events based on recent findings describing how a small fraction of committed individuals may trigger behavioural cascades. First, we operationalize the concept of individual commitment in financial discussions. Second, we show that the increase of commitment within Reddit predated the initial surge in price. Third, we reveal that initial committed users occupied a central position in the network of Reddit conversations. Finally, we show that the social identity of the broader Reddit community grew as the collective action unfolded. These findings shed light on financial collective action, as several observers anticipate it will grow in importance.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lucchini, Lorenzo","Aiello, Luca Maria","Alessandretti, Laura","De Francisci Morales, Gianmarco","Starnini, Michele","Baronchelli, Andrea"],"cuid":["S02-087.97-033269"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Baronchelli, Andrea"],"country":["Italy","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Bocconi University","City, University of London","Fondazione Bruno Kessler","Institute for Scientific Interchange Foundation","University College London"],"keyword":["Collective action frame","Networks","Reddit","Social media","WallStreetBets","committed minorities","gme"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"from reddit to wall street the role of committed minorities in financial collective action","src":["dtu","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.211488","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["L.M.A. is partly supported by the Carlsberg Foundation through the COCOONS project (CF21-0432). G.D.F.M. and M.S. acknowledge support from Intesa San Paolo Innovation Center. A.B. acknowledges the 100683EPID Project “Global Health Security Academic Research Coalition” SCH-00001-3391. L.M.A. is partly supported by the Carlsberg Foundation through the COCOONS project (CF21-0432). G.D.F.M. and M.S. acknowledge support from Intesa San Paolo Innovation Center. A.B. acknowledges the 100683EPID Project ?Global Health Security Academic Research Coalition? SCH-00001-3391."],"id":"S02-087.97-033269","identifier":[{"id":"85128912255","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35425623","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8984357","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["dtu-1f44035d-e67e-4e8d-b2b8-6ca887247169","itu-cfc27d2c-0047-4a17-b75f-ed9fabf95273"],"keyword":["Collective action frame","Coordination dynamics","Networks","Social media","committed minorities","gme","reddit","wallstreetbets"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D703EB62124EE401B261D83ECB4E28B6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90810431/rsos.211488.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/275918025/rsos.211488.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.211488","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"bocconi university","loc":["Ita