{"abstract":"The continuity hypothesis of dreams suggests that the content of dreams is continuous with the dreamer's waking experiences. Given the unprecedented nature of the experiences during COVID-19, we studied the continuity hypothesis in the context of the pandemic. We implemented a deep-learning algorithm that can extract mentions of medical conditions from text and applied it to two datasets collected during the pandemic: 2888 dream reports (dreaming life experiences), and 57 milion tweets (waking life experiences) mentioning the pandemic. The health expressions common to both sets were typical COVID-19 symptoms (e.g. cough, fever and anxiety), suggesting that dreams reflected people's real-world experiences. The health expressions that distinguished the two sets reflected differences in thought processes: expressions in waking life reflected a linear and logical thought process and, as such, described realistic symptoms or related disorders (e.g. nasal pain, SARS, H1N1); those in dreaming life reflected a thought process closer to the visual and emotional spheres and, as such, described either conditions unrelated to the virus (e.g. maggots, deformities, snake bites), or conditions of surreal nature (e.g. teeth falling out, body crumbling into sand). Our results confirm that dream reports represent an understudied yet valuable source of people's health experiences in the real world.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Scepanovic, Sanja","Aiello, Luca Maria","Barrett, Deirdre","Quercia, Daniele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383200"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard University","Nokia Bell Labs"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Dreams"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"epidemic dreams dreaming about health during the covid 000000000019 pandemic","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.211080","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383200","identifier":[{"id":"85124179732","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8352beff-bba8-47d6-a601-254e5cbf01f7"],"keyword":["COVID-19","dreams"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7F00D689504D7AB81D5D5BD8A6BEDE74"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90811763/rsos.211080.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.211080","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"nokia bell labs","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Nokia Bell Labs","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sanja","key":"scepanovicpau","last":"Scepanovic","name":"Scepanovic, Sanja","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Deirdre","key":"barrettpau","last":"Barrett","name":"Barrett, Deirdre","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniele","key":"querciapau","last":"Quercia","name":"Quercia, Daniele","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1098/rsos.211080","issn":["20545703"],"issue":"1","journal":"Royal Society Open Science","pages":"1-12","paperid":"211080","status":"p","title":"Royal Society Open Science","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124179732"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8352beff-bba8-47d6-a601-254e5cbf01f7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Epidemic dreams: dreaming about health during the COVID-19 pandemic","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383200","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We quantify Non Fungible Token (NFT) rarity and investigate how it impacts market behaviour by analysing a dataset of 3.7M transactions collected between January 2018 and June 2022, involving 1.4M NFTs distributed across 410 collections. First, we consider the rarity of an NFT based on the set of human-readable attributes it possesses and show that most collections present heterogeneous rarity patterns, with few rare NFTs and a large number of more common ones. Then, we analyze market performance and show that, on average, rarer NFTs: (i) sell for higher prices, (ii) are traded less frequently, (iii) guarantee higher returns on investment, and (iv) are less risky, i.e., less prone to yield negative returns. We anticipate that these findings will be of interest to researchers as well as NFT creators, collectors, and traders.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Mekacher, Amin","Bracci, Alberto","Nadini, Matthieu","Martino, Mauro","Alessandretti, Laura","Aiello, Luca Maria","Baronchelli, Andrea"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-032905"],"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Baronchelli, Andrea"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City, University of London","Elliptic Enterprises Ltd.","IBM","IBM Research","University College London","University of London"],"keyword":["Rarity","nft","opensea"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"heterogeneous rarity patterns drive price dynamics in nft collections","src":["dtu","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-17922-5","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The research was partly supported by The Alan Turing Institute. L.M.A. acknowledges the support from the Carlsberg Foundation through the COCOONS project (CF21-0432)."],"id":"S02-100.00-032905","identifier":[{"id":"85135987011","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9381539","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"35974056","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-0e4698df-fcb3-4641-aaa8-145463502a09","itu-f85dd3ec-ee27-4011-91b3-45a6bb299983"],"keyword":["nft","opensea","rarity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-89555679267DF77AC870FC504A6FF3B1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-17922-5","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90810736/s41598_022_17922_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/288228932/s41598_022_17922_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/297691361/s41598_022_20027_8.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of London","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"city university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"City, University of London","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"elliptic enterprises ltd","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Elliptic Enterprises Ltd.","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"ibm","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"IBM","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"ibm research","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"IBM Research","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"dtu techni